[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a complete, step‑by‑step recipe for adding a **hillshade** layer that is driven by a **raster‑DEM** source to a MapLibre GL JS map.  
Everything you need is in the code snippets, plus a short checklist of gotchas (CORS, projection, style ordering, etc.).

---

## 1. Prerequisites

| What you need | Why it matters |
|---------------|----------------|
| **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 2.0** (or any recent 1.x version) | `type: "hillshade"` is supported from the first release. |
| A **raster‑DEM** tile service (e.g. Mapbox Terrain‑RGB, Open‑Topography, your own MBTiles server) that serves **`application/x-protobuf`** tiles (or PNG/JPEG if you set `encoding: "terrarium"`). | The hillshade layer reads elevation values from the DEM tiles. |
| **CORS** enabled on the DEM endpoint (or proxy it). | The browser must be allowed to fetch the tiles. |
| (Optional) **Terrain source** if you also want 3‑D terrain. | Hillshade works independently, but you can combine both for a richer look. |

---

## 2. Add the raster‑DEM source to the style

```js
// 1️⃣ Create the map (basic example)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // HTML element id
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

// 2️⃣ When the style is loaded, add the DEM source
map.on('style.load', () => {
  map.addSource('dem', {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    // ──► Choose ONE of the following URL patterns ────────────────────────
    // 1) Mapbox Terrain‑RGB (requires a Mapbox token)
    // url: 'mapbox://mapbox.terrain-rgb',
    // 2) Open‑Topography (public DEM tiles)
    // url: 'https://opentopography.org/tiles/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
    // 3) Your own MBTiles/TileServer endpoint (replace {z}/{x}/{y})
    url: 'https://my-server.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
    // ──► Common options ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    tileSize: 256,               // 256 or 512 – must match the source tiles
    maxzoom: 14,                 // highest zoom you have tiles for
    // If your tiles are in **Terrarium** format (PNG with 0‑255 values):
    // encoding: 'terrarium',
    // If they are Mapbox Terrain‑RGB (default):
    // encoding: 'mapbox',
    // (omit `encoding` for the default “mapbox”)
    // Optional: give the DEM a name for debugging
    attribution: '© My DEM provider'
  });
});
```

### What the fields mean

| Property | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `type: "raster-dem"` | Tells MapLibre the source contains elevation data, not ordinary imagery. |
| `url` | Tile URL template. Use `{z}/{x}/{y}` placeholders. |
| `tileSize` | Must match the tile size of the service (most are 256 px). |
| `maxzoom` | Prevents the client from requesting tiles that don’t exist. |
| `encoding` | `"mapbox"` for Terrain‑RGB (default), `"terrarium"` for the older Terrarium PNG format. |
| `attribution` | Optional but good practice. |

---

## 3. Add the hillshade layer

```js
// 3️⃣ Add the hillshade layer *after* the DEM source exists
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'dem',               // matches the source id above
  // Optional: you can limit the layer to a specific zoom range
  minzoom: 0,
  maxzoom: 14,
  // ──► Styling options (all are numbers in the range 0‑1) ─────────────────────
  paint: {
    // Light direction (azimuth) – 0 = north, 0.5 = south, etc.
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 0.5,
    // Light intensity (0 = dark, 1 = full sun)
    'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
    // Shading intensity for the three components
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,          // overall contrast boost
    'hillshade-shadow-color': '#000000',   // dark side
    'hillshade-highlight-color': '#ffffff'// bright side
  }
});
```

#### Paint properties you can tweak

| Property | Typical range | Effect |
|----------|---------------|--------|
| `hillshade-illumination-direction` | 0 – 1 (0 = north) | Controls where the “sun” shines. |
| `hillshade-illumination-anchor` | `"map"` or `"viewport"` | `"map"` rotates the light with the map; `"viewport"` keeps it fixed to the screen. |
| `hillshade-exaggeration` | 0 – 1 (or >1 for extra contrast) | Boosts the depth of shadows/highlights. |
| `hillshade-shadow-color` / `hillshade-highlight-color` | Any CSS color | Color of the dark and bright sides. |
| `hillshade-shadow-color` and `hillshade-highlight-color` can also be expressed as `rgba()` to add transparency. |

---

## 4. (Optional) Combine with a terrain source for 3‑D

If you also want a 3‑D terrain surface, add a **terrain source** (the same DEM can be reused) and enable it:

```js
// Add a terrain source (same DEM can be reused)
map.addSource('terrain', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://my-server.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 14
});

// Enable terrain rendering
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1.5 });
```

The hillshade layer will automatically shade the terrain surface, giving a realistic “relief” effect.

---

## 5. Full working example (HTML)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre Hillshade Demo</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 60,          // tilt to see the hillshade
    bearing: -20
  });

  map.on('style.load', () => {
    // DEM source
    map.addSource('dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://my-server.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 14
    });

    // Hillshade layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 0.5,
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.6,
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)'
      }
    });

    // Optional 3‑D terrain
    map.addSource('terrain', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://my-server.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 14
    });
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1.2 });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser (or serve it via a local web server) and you’ll see a sloped, shaded landscape. Adjust the `paint` values to taste.

---

## 6. Checklist & Common Pitfalls

| ✅ Checklist | ❌ What goes wrong if you miss it |
|--------------|-----------------------------------|
| **CORS** on the DEM tiles (header `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` or your domain) | Tiles are blocked → hillshade layer stays invisible, console shows “Cross‑origin request blocked”. |
| **`type: "raster-dem"`** (not `"raster"`). | MapLibre treats the source as imagery → no elevation → hillshade is flat/white. |
| **Matching `tileSize`** with the server (256 vs 512). | Tiles are mis‑aligned → visual artifacts or missing tiles. |
| **Correct `encoding`** (`"mapbox"` for Terrain‑RGB, `"terrarium"` for PNG). | Elevation values are interpreted incorrectly → hillshade looks like a noisy mess. |
| **Layer order** – place the hillshade **below** any label or symbol layers you want to see. | Labels get hidden behind the hillshade. |
| **Zoom range** – `maxzoom` on the source must be ≥ the map’s current zoom. | At high zoom you’ll see “no data” tiles and the hillshade disappears. |
| **`localIdeographFontFamily`** (if you also have labels with CJK) – unrelated to hillshade but can affect label visibility. | CJK labels may disappear if the default local‑font fallback is disabled. |

---

## 7. Further Reading

* **MapLibre GL JS style spec – `raster-dem` source**: <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/sources/#raster-dem>
* **Hillshade layer reference**: <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/style-spec/layers/#hillshade>
* **Terrain‑RGB vs Terrarium**: <https://github.com/mapbox/raster-dem#terrain-rgb-and-terrarium>
* **Performance tip** – keep `hillshade-exaggeration` modest; very high values can make the map look “over‑shaded” and increase GPU load.

---

### TL;DR

1. **Add a `raster-dem` source** pointing at your DEM tiles.  
2. **Add a `hillshade` layer** that uses that source and tweak its `paint` properties.  
3. (Optional) **Enable 3‑D terrain** with the same DEM for a full relief effect.  
4. Verify CORS, tile size, and encoding; then enjoy a nicely shaded terrain map.
